#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087823 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087427 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "f73207f4-4661-55f1-acfb-cc5fe6ba9316" :question/math-academy-id "e-1221" :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y=5(3^x)-3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y=3^{x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/1c/1cd695e6f1fab35a692fde9003d452862db33a894c23657eb87f8d773de8cbb9.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=5(3^x).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $5.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,5),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/1c/1c92ea5f3cbfd40bd0dc384b152f6166c27400dbedd93a9c8333195950e7f9c3.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=5(3^x)-3.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $3$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,5)$ to $(0,2),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-3.$\n\n![](images/d8/d8e8cdccd58a9f1f21c8c2bf0a4a8c08e9c263c4fe91cc92e2bee334de057116.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513420 :question-generator/id #uuid "35bd8dd3-54c5-5fe0-8635-7b694fdfe873" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186087823.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2c04986-70ee-5052-9ec5-142496777802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1c/1c5c95216d03c539a31f8b81b21dd874cb2288c15a5c83b978fd45433f30353a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085351} {:answer/id #uuid "e5acf14e-9406-5c10-aec2-1d303303e0d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/15/152a5356acdcca5917a01c87b96349f9a587214392bb2e416e11d04e74a3e007.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085352} {:answer/id #uuid "43cd4169-a078-5896-a542-81a9e4dc79bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3f/3ffbfc3a208bb839f2699665ac0e6ffefad9f301158d4c3aafceb828a1f23527.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085353} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae6a940-4764-56e3-afcc-cb11469803e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/11/1172d95a494114455636188930f09fc1f78e23a4f5820244a5ef777b48c61633.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085354} {:answer/id #uuid "64c969a9-8a2d-5cad-a691-124efd83daf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7c/7c428fdf4f66ce7c80d64daa714af81befb2908e1da1aac5580425ae2425730c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085355}] :answer-field/id #uuid "872ff259-84a8-5c41-8343-8fd5a0f06a93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085350}] :question/id #uuid "2d06cd58-841e-5527-a294-4dae48a373bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-26910" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y = 4(6^{x}) - 2$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088126 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0718a36d-396f-5368-85ba-5007af7edbf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295300} {:answer/id #uuid "102df500-4571-5550-8394-ccf1449c0d36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(11,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295361} {:answer/id #uuid "3d7e6108-fabc-5a0c-af99-2e167084609b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295621} {:answer/id #uuid "4ace8761-2cf1-5067-a981-dd24a6032050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295692} {:answer/id #uuid "9774f025-3ad5-5dd7-8c8e-12ea3987a542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ace8761-2cf1-5067-a981-dd24a6032050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295692} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f42c0d6-5043-56d2-b372-7673a9d172a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9e76cd0-6e63-52b6-ad92-c0e43ec54487" :question/math-academy-id "q-30270" :question/problem "For the curve $y=5({6}^{x})+2,$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={6}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=5({6}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=5\\cdot 1=5.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $2$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=5+2=7.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5({6}^{0})+2 \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1+2 \\\\  & =5+2 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=5({6}^{x})+2$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,7).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284658 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18b5bc36-e007-593f-8d2c-a16b08fc3d18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281912} {:answer/id #uuid "60bb1b4f-6b88-526c-9dab-0b8304b3f942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282510} {:answer/id #uuid "99796e47-234a-5f62-9f0a-956c0cd56a93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283024} {:answer/id #uuid "99e0b67c-9be5-5fe2-ba59-a19376e720ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283028} {:answer/id #uuid "aa64d2f2-0944-5be4-be52-ad85329db1c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99e0b67c-9be5-5fe2-ba59-a19376e720ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283028} :answer-field/id #uuid "a670f1f8-5c9f-5514-826c-a3d0cc3426e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59cc81c5-fc9d-517e-99cf-c3bfb1a3b17a" :question/math-academy-id "q-170594" :question/problem "For the curve $y=1.5({4}^{x})+1,$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={4}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=1.5({4}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=1.5\\cdot 1=1.5.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $1$ unit up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=1.5+1=2.5.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1.5({4}^{0})+1 \\\\  & =1.5\\cdot 1+1 \\\\  & =1.5+1 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=1.5({4}^{x})+1,$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,2.5).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0265ab4b-e69c-58cd-bb39-877b73f3298f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,11.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295275} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab493a4-b37f-5582-a8b9-4570920c001f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10.5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295861} {:answer/id #uuid "a1678182-8bd9-5b40-83a4-560b3c92dfe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296126} {:answer/id #uuid "ecdd579a-68d8-530f-aec2-948295be5064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296522} {:answer/id #uuid "f541b6c1-d1ab-5c71-9e34-c29b7b48861a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5.5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1678182-8bd9-5b40-83a4-560b3c92dfe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296126} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0972749-6df3-5836-b156-61b2f017bcaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "91d38446-45fe-5f55-b13c-726fa96fa8c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-30269" :question/problem "For the curve $y=2.5({1.5}^{x})+8$, find the point of intersection with the $y-$axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={1.5}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=2.5({1.5}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=2.5\\cdot 1=2.5.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $8$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=2.5+8=10.5.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2.5({1.5}^{0})+8 \\\\  & =2.5\\cdot 1+8 \\\\  & =2.5+8 \\\\  & =10.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2.5({1.5}^{x})+8$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,10.5).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e5493f0-49e5-5ac0-a64b-444f26facf5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295811} {:answer/id #uuid "cedf3ffd-b468-57c5-a1fb-5644942d8367" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296352} {:answer/id #uuid "d228956c-dabb-5adf-b1f0-eea00077c044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296375} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e90daa-a470-5b92-aeb7-f90fc7f25a72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296492} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3169b4-14de-5f3c-837e-5d327959c0c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d228956c-dabb-5adf-b1f0-eea00077c044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296375} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ef5eb88-b39d-569b-bc4c-949ed152d723" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fda2bca-a959-55aa-9acd-07aede112347" :question/math-academy-id "q-30873" :question/problem "For the curve $y=1.5({10}^{x})-8.5$, find the point of intersection with the $y-$axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={10}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=1.5({10}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=1.5\\cdot 1=1.5.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $8.5$ units down, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=1.5-8.5=-7.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1.5({10}^{0})-8.5 \\\\  & =1.5\\cdot 1-8.5 \\\\  & =1.5-8.5 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=1.5({10}^{x})-8.5$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-7).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16cf5275-48a4-5d52-bfe0-a5c956f020ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ee/eed5f150b826a708834eb9d7c4595cbdcc0113842fc0b172c1cf11e65681b199.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336246} {:answer/id #uuid "1accd3cf-7928-5942-9ce7-0ac1d86bacc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d5/d5c68724af1732ee023599ce2969c165dfc68b8e198e14cebbc1262bbf1bd881.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336280} {:answer/id #uuid "4282c634-a61a-54b3-9818-ffa50eb3be52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/75/752c0102b1c019f5bc13673b0107627bf58954fb5ef5b80d3b43e33e5f46f55d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336661} {:answer/id #uuid "8aed9c65-0e6d-56b4-abe0-e8d553ce98e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/96/960ebb31bb1330c7def20222f012e2c89d0f2f4f588cabb914c42c60571dfcdc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337286} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4ecd2b-a163-51b1-add4-86835f9e1935" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/58/580a0d2a241a867159827d1570d20bb120e70297fef4b68c103fc580bbb2b224.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8aed9c65-0e6d-56b4-abe0-e8d553ce98e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/96/960ebb31bb1330c7def20222f012e2c89d0f2f4f588cabb914c42c60571dfcdc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337286} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fd8ca31-a85e-5970-a1f2-11e22bec8157" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3548d09b-2351-5856-9855-c3a656fa374f" :question/math-academy-id "q-7121" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=2({3}^{x})-4?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={3}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/1c/1cd695e6f1fab35a692fde9003d452862db33a894c23657eb87f8d773de8cbb9.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=2({3}^{x}).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $2.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,2),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/5a/5a9871ac900e85c9f47118f6233b2e7f094af010dcb73e54221cadac01ce1644.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=2({3}^{x})-4.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $4$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,2)$ to $(0,-2),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-4.$\n\n![](images/89/89a7ee023e8d4232b617052efdc9844cb00b2e3f05968119cea11c89242ca61a.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513368 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "185b5c03-502c-5e51-81ed-67a2ae4c4909" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ac/ac7cf47371cac874fb490a02f833eba98fca3276de9bfa6f2e4fa0bfd8220aa7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513358} {:answer/id #uuid "388739ab-9f33-5b20-9615-f6f44d85cb02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c8/c8df54c2a83b1309977876709024000db2607ea2c8f4721d34e1dbd318e005b4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513367} {:answer/id #uuid "bd1a4ba7-18e4-5a47-ba0d-48353a165bfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e0/e086238ec04e05c9041b2d383de331891d78111633290555e8ce5a7977abb38d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513389} {:answer/id #uuid "c42f57c3-fe67-506f-b44e-da07e41cc2d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0d/0d5ef83c31fbb18c28ffa632e310c6a136e3ae094c927975020ec4c840abdf8e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513394} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9c1588-08ee-520c-b32b-297f4edb5134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8ba15c6661242ed21a59fd5fa0c1513281844433518063e2ef2a6d527ec9e824.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd1a4ba7-18e4-5a47-ba0d-48353a165bfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e0/e086238ec04e05c9041b2d383de331891d78111633290555e8ce5a7977abb38d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513389} :answer-field/id #uuid "f242afd2-b1c2-5dee-b46d-cefed40a3bd8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "39949ab3-d4b6-56dc-b156-2489f106ad21" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=1.5({6}^{x})+2.5?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={6}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/1b/1b936cf103a607a6b26a060ba747717062698ab005910923f36d54f1d002f119.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=1.5({6}^{x}).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $1.5.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,1.5),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/04/04f703b2efc93aa95d297bf78f357c1e47118a8bfffdbf1d88601844ee8f51d2.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=1.5({6}^{x})+2.5.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $2.5$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1.5)$ to $(0,4),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=2.5.$\n\n![](images/e0/e086238ec04e05c9041b2d383de331891d78111633290555e8ce5a7977abb38d.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "052c68d3-8c32-5683-b862-d94a7bb06d65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dd/ddce7ab24a84961a05cef831aece2ef6e6dae67d92b3d8bfdbd996adf801fbb9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513351} {:answer/id #uuid "341822af-7f85-5cca-86d1-9c4daebff9b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/45/45b8143a46420bcd29ab8e3d74fed4bedd5bf08ab4ada00c383589ab7afe37e7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513366} {:answer/id #uuid "bd2e42bf-63f2-52e9-ba51-ab728cad0640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f1/f1e35939274b521c122fafbef1ae4cb715ffc4368d2f6073d76a551760fa749b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513390} {:answer/id #uuid "c9947fd3-92bc-5715-bbd1-69ad478c388f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d4/d451eb926025f5c2f4f6dde19abfa3f1183b15ecd2dd10a577f19226d0a62da2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513398} {:answer/id #uuid "f2278f5d-342f-50ad-9d84-83d792df1152" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/43/433636c140f2fee6e606882a4cde1345c3b01040022f1eb4b8c86ddb0464d68f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9947fd3-92bc-5715-bbd1-69ad478c388f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d4/d451eb926025f5c2f4f6dde19abfa3f1183b15ecd2dd10a577f19226d0a62da2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513398} :answer-field/id #uuid "552950ae-af6a-5e34-8ee2-7b7490ddea5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "48d1be08-87e1-5824-9a0b-420b3028233f" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=1.5({10}^{x})+1.5?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={10}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/df/dfba05178209ded9fe3d35eba7d647734a331a4968c42edd1b4fdc14439aa902.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=1.5({10}^{x}).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $1.5.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,1.5),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/00/006b8305ac136b5ca1ca47feff165d72de432aebb7f39e855a8f96aaeb20fc41.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=1.5({10}^{x})+1.5.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $1.5$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1.5)$ to $(0,3),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=1.5.$\n\n![](images/d4/d451eb926025f5c2f4f6dde19abfa3f1183b15ecd2dd10a577f19226d0a62da2.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "082d9271-1b37-5ab5-be6d-7707a2094f66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ad/ad6aab8b1f1c09478ee422dcb0871274174dd3907ab31f250657b035d56d9e5b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513352} {:answer/id #uuid "17aed16e-d180-583d-925d-9518de27b784" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3b/3b1cc1edeee7de5b8a324ab8e64d5d0119da94e8aa734cefb0fac838aaa3cded.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513356} {:answer/id #uuid "cdaaf681-61c3-55a9-b549-77a77268a11b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/22/22c9b5417d5675a583b08c97f9a859eae0f