ges/32/3283f0e4e42c8525667e77d983776f28183961540950ca0d45416af7c19ba422.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=1.5({2}^{x})+0.5.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $0.5$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1.5)$ to $(0,2),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=0.5.$\n\n![](images/e6/e6ba39f00ec3e15b528cf987bcaceb5328c00b70232eea48c94435e981eda4ef.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513405 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c71384d-e28c-5acc-96cb-e079db50fb4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b9/b993208a3b5d908cffa76ae69d8affe092e27744e77a4a923d31cae2402537f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513354} {:answer/id #uuid "6c3a4d15-2e63-546e-914e-7b2d23c6bd94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/73/73b63bb63379228300acda5f33034e7f1e852c9a21f71bc2ee47d72b4940256a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513376} {:answer/id #uuid "85b74457-9f28-5c91-923c-a2e9d1171e35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6b/6bd3286cfa6646d6958d1b847c62472af6c82b6be3006d726de18b6539d02aef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513382} {:answer/id #uuid "c58baa83-ecbd-585c-bb55-08440ebf0902" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6a/6a958c436a5a31af4676b1dd2747d29055dd957dbb04468fa679b5ba62407e56.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513396} {:answer/id #uuid "d1681c71-b491-5ee5-a872-342bb61314e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fa/fa0b76868d2927e65276cd72975391c296607bb653be8821d913afe0aa739dcc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c58baa83-ecbd-585c-bb55-08440ebf0902" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6a/6a958c436a5a31af4676b1dd2747d29055dd957dbb04468fa679b5ba62407e56.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513396} :answer-field/id #uuid "2df94396-56be-546d-bcd2-b31d955d9c7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db5b0c8b-dd31-584b-8bb7-f42a116b6bda" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=5({6}^{x})-8?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={6}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/1b/1b936cf103a607a6b26a060ba747717062698ab005910923f36d54f1d002f119.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=5({6}^{x}).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $5.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,5),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/85/85d83e3770cd6995bb95be06df90379f89777d0e322d4029c698e4fe2e3e0848.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=5({6}^{x})-8.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $8$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,5)$ to $(0,-3),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-8.$\n\n![](images/6a/6a958c436a5a31af4676b1dd2747d29055dd957dbb04468fa679b5ba62407e56.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513408 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09f7fd95-5b85-572f-83fb-d96bf9a648d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/22/22cf3584de8a6ffc895bf105785ded57a77c466300e0ca59dbabefbe76e2b1a8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513353} {:answer/id #uuid "22dd3e30-3ed4-5e4a-93ed-686c845202cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ae/aed02a04b49c9a428422c53894df87391e6d8f882e11935a659ac714ff63d241.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513361} {:answer/id #uuid "22f0e67a-6c35-54f8-b73d-9ee7388f3642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ec/ecb36e3cfea543fae67d0968979f53200816850227cea55ae71abc0b877967bd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513362} {:answer/id #uuid "2975fe54-f579-5c20-bd1f-a8b4b6fb33a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ee/ee03fb1f492088182ef34b4402dc79654aec411a868c4fb60f91d9a950502f81.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513363} {:answer/id #uuid "78d565a1-f15a-5183-b7ce-81ba20369c21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/da/dab44a0fed3aa4ba001b48eba6ad55a6fd9baae62af23b967e3fb54b52da7d43.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22dd3e30-3ed4-5e4a-93ed-686c845202cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ae/aed02a04b49c9a428422c53894df87391e6d8f882e11935a659ac714ff63d241.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513361} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2f87263-2196-5d2a-933a-46a206fb0f1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e8eb9941-3ad1-5843-95c0-9bfc608a7ab0" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=8({1.5}^{x})+4?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={1.5}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/8e/8eef43ea7539e0932505e1a59487ca0ce87dc63c463c8a7b51265aa5286db6d0.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=8({1.5}^{x}).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $8.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,8),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/2b/2b8bcef6718ccb06bfbca4b65b41989511107104dede1328f1031ff8b7426241.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=8({1.5}^{x})+4.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $4$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,8)$ to $(0,12),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$\n\n![](images/ae/aed02a04b49c9a428422c53894df87391e6d8f882e11935a659ac714ff63d241.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ce691b7-7119-5ae4-8d24-fe227eb4085b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/50/50cf0261f7df41dac9c89e687a3c0d317021642fdfd3f1ed8035f31714ff587e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513369} {:answer/id #uuid "96c4a797-8f4a-5a45-adc7-3c7d2e24e31d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ce/ce5f63c06cfd6d321f7cbd8f17e7078eabb7a8e89473d475ec6efbebdef77b8a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513384} {:answer/id #uuid "a89c38e7-7e79-5e02-8753-b49643451f21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/74/748d7970553a8d054c415882a443e61bcd2f858da92079d537bc3574e36c28b9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513385} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4f6e82-8d05-5586-af53-9a1d342d33a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/53/536c3a1bdd9993305910750db03bd79e63dc8e76401fb41abe762ec40411cb5a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513399} {:answer/id #uuid "e47e1f35-1ff9-5bad-b94c-423d06fb0e70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4d/4daf5a1bc93f36540384f37d292f6790620e20ba521c985af0f0db5255bdfa2c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a89c38e7-7e79-5e02-8753-b49643451f21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/74/748d7970553a8d054c415882a443e61bcd2f858da92079d537bc3574e36c28b9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513385} :answer-field/id #uuid "55c154b8-3776-5778-9cc9-260a3cdc7666" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fb0cfba4-eeeb-5c5b-b7db-97b99d0eccb2" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=6.5({3}^{x})-0.5?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={3}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/6a/6a7938d23d1de5e91d734afd9a930d1d8cc1f82407dd484177d3f7852a27a915.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=6.5({3}^{x}).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $6.5.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,6.5),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/8f/8f8c99a7d9b4ea251c25da35ffce56ca1a8e94225dcab7075bf687e192ad0a09.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=6.5({3}^{x})-0.5.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $0.5$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,6.5)$ to $(0,6),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-0.5.$\n\n![](images/74/748d7970553a8d054c415882a443e61bcd2f858da92079d537bc3574e36c28b9.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "023974b6-d52e-54a2-a035-bc3d44ad09c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513350} {:answer/id #uuid "13874e8e-5fc8-5da2-8595-dd066e6d59ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513355} {:answer/id #uuid "6fdd468f-762a-56b4-90a6-0b31e08b7022" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513377} {:answer/id #uuid "da4cffe5-8815-56cc-8624-d4f5e905eedb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513404} {:answer/id #uuid "f50698c5-ac01-5deb-86f7-eb5a03f596c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fdd468f-762a-56b4-90a6-0b31e08b7022" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513377} :answer-field/id #uuid "c52165a1-0104-5320-b88e-6d8bbf0b6944" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff4b88d8-da28-5f54-8746-3c0f00566a0c" :question/problem "For the curve $y=6({10}^{x})-8,$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={10}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=6({10}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=6\\cdot 1=6.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $8$ units down, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=6-8=-2.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6({10}^{0})-8 \\\\  & =6\\cdot 1-8 \\\\  & =6-8 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=6({10}^{x})-8,$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-2).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}]}
